# A take-off from water in a flying boat: afloat to five hundred feet.
#
#   name TEXT...                   what the lesson is called
#   teaches CLASS                  which class of aircraft it is for
#   stage TEXT...                  a stage, in the order flown
#   do TEXT...                     what to do at this stage
#   until PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE     what ends the stage
#   hold PROPERTY LOW HIGH TEXT... a band that must hold for the whole stage
#   need PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE TEXT...  what must be true by the stage's end
#
# with `#` beginning a comment; src/sim/lesson.hpp owns the format.
#
# **The technique is the one the sources give.** The run is held at the
# hull's running attitude - over the hump, where she plows, and on to the step,
# where she planes - and the nose raised from the rotation speed until she is
# clear: Arthur Gouge's account of the Short S.23's take-off tests (Flight,
# 17 December 1936, p. 649), whose running attitude of eight degrees and
# rotation at sixty knots `assets/figures/short_s23.xml` records; the FAA's
# Seaplane, Skiplane, and Float/Ski Equipped Helicopter Operations Handbook
# (FAA-H-8083-23), chapter 4, on the planing attitude and on porpoising, which
# an attitude too low or too high on the step sets off; and the FlightGear
# model's own handling notes - "keep the control column back, take off pitch
# is about 9 deg", flap no more than a third - which its author says are his
# reading of the records, not the aeroplane's instructions.
#
# **There is no runway to keep straight on**, but there is a heading, and a
# swing on the water is a waterloop in the making: the heading is watched as
# a landplane's is.
#
# **The step has an attitude, and a fault either side of it.** The FAA's
# handbook (chapter 4, "Porpoising") has a seaplane plane smoothly only in a
# moderately tolerant range of pitch: held too low, the bow digs in and she
# porpoises, in oscillations that grow until she noses in; held too high, she
# porpoises the other way and can be thrown off the water too slow. The run is
# two stages, so that the band watches the step and not the hump below it,
# where she plows nose-high at any attitude. Flown by the AI pilot the S.23
# planes at 7.7 to 12.2 degrees; with the stick held forward on the step she
# porpoises between -5 and +7 degrees and never leaves the water. Held fully
# back she planes as by the book: the water, not the elevator, sets her
# attitude on the step in this model, so the nose-high fault is not flown.
#
# **The bands are measured**, with room: flown by the AI pilot the Short S.23
# comes off the water at 78 knots - the FlightGear notes' "minimum getting off
# speed" is 78 mph - holds -0.8 to 15.6 degrees of pitch in the initial climb
# and climbs away at 99 to 104 knots against the 100 it publishes.

name Take-off from water in a flying boat
teaches seaplane

stage Over the hump
do Throttles open smoothly and evenly to full
do Hold her straight with rudder and throttles, the wings level
do Stick back as she plows, and let her climb over the hump on to the step
hold lesson/turned-deg -20 20 Keep her straight on the take-off run
need fcs/throttle-cmd-norm >= 0.90 Open the throttles fully for the take-off
until velocities/vc-kts >= rotate-15

stage On the step
do Hold the planing attitude on the step, neither nose high nor nose low
do Keep her straight, and let her fly off at the rotation speed
hold lesson/turned-deg -20 20 Keep her straight on the take-off run
hold attitude/theta-deg 6.0 14.0 Hold the planing attitude on the step
need velocities/vc-kts >= rotate-5 Let her reach the rotation speed before lifting her off
until position/h-agl-ft >= 20

stage The initial climb
do Ease her off the step and hold the climbing attitude
hold attitude/theta-deg -5.0 20.0 Hold the climbing attitude
until position/h-agl-ft >= 200

stage Climbing away
do Climb straight ahead at the climbing speed
hold velocities/vc-kts climb-15 climb+15 Hold the climbing speed
until position/h-agl-ft >= 500
